problems.problem:
Let $A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I$ be nine points in space such that
$A, B, C, D, E$, in this order, are the vertices of a regular pentagon of side $1$;
$A, B, F, G, H$, in this order, are the vertices of a regular pentagon of side $1$;
$G, F, C, D, I$, in this order, are the vertices of a regular pentagon of side $1$.
Determine the lengths of the sides of triangle $EHI$.

Let $n \geq 3$ be a positive integer. Determine, as a function of $n$, how many triples of sets $(A, B, C)$ satisfy the conditions:
• $A$, $B$ and $C$ are pairwise disjoint, that is, $A \cap B = A \cap C = B \cap C = \emptyset$;
• $A \cup B \cup C = \{1, 2, \ldots, n\}$;
• the sum of the elements of $A$, the sum of the elements of $B$ and the sum of the elements of $C$ leave the same remainder when divided by $3$.
Note: one or more of the sets may be empty.

When the natural numbers are written one after another in increasing order, an infinite sequence of digits $123456789101112\ldots$ is obtained. The number formed by the first $k$ digits of this sequence is denoted $A_k$. Prove that for every positive integer $n$ there exists a positive integer $m$ that simultaneously satisfies the following three conditions:
(i) $n$ divides $A_m$;
(ii) $n$ divides $m$;
(iii) $n$ divides the sum of the digits of $A_m$.
